"""Prepared (preparation-only) reference for ``nested_tensor_from_mask``.

Injected through the project's existing ``--override`` path in place of
``flag_gems._nested_tensor_from_mask`` (accuracy suite) and resolved through the
benchmark override registry under ``nested_tensor_from_mask`` (benchmark
suite).  This is a reference-as-solution preparation tool, not an optimized
candidate.

Oracle choice: the frozen master test itself defines the expected value as
``torch.ops.aten._nested_tensor_from_mask(ref_t, ref_mask, mask_check=...)``
(``tests/test_nested_tensor_from_mask.py``).  The reference therefore delegates
to that same native ATen kernel, which is the only implementation that can
reproduce the frozen assertions on legacy (``torch.strided``) NestedTensor
metadata (layout, ``_nested_tensor_size``, ``_nested_tensor_strides``,
``_nested_tensor_storage_offsets``).

Contract preserved:

* candidate ABI of the frozen Definition (``definitions/nested_tensor_from_mask.json``):
  ``(t, mask, mask_check=True)``, ``mask_check`` optional and defaulting to
  ``True``; both positional and keyword calls are accepted.
* inputs are used as given: no dtype upcast, no CPU round trip, no clone, no
  input rewrite; the returned nested tensor stays on ``t.dtype``/``t.device``.
* ``effects``: neither ``t`` nor ``mask`` is mutated, and the result is not an
  alias of an input.
* the ``mask_check=True`` rejection path is preserved verbatim: a mask with a
  False-then-True gap raises the native ``RuntimeError`` (the frozen test
  ``test_nested_tensor_from_mask_mask_check_raises`` asserts exactly that).
* no import of ``flag_gems`` and no call to the operator under test, so this
  wrapper cannot re-enter the injected/registered operator as its own oracle.
"""

import torch

_ATEN_OP = torch.ops.aten._nested_tensor_from_mask


def run(t, mask, mask_check=True):
    """Return the legacy NestedTensor built by the native ATen reference."""
    return _ATEN_OP(t, mask, mask_check=mask_check)


# Aliases so an override spec without an explicit function name still resolves
# to the same entrypoint (default func_name is the override op name).
nested_tensor_from_mask = run
_nested_tensor_from_mask = run
